# Line-ending policy for FinRecon.
#
# This repo has no prior .gitattributes, so checkout line endings were only
# ever governed by each developer's local core.autocrlf. That let two
# incompatible representations of the same LF-stored git blobs exist:
# CRLF on a Windows checkout (autocrlf=true) and LF on a Linux checkout
# (Railway, CI, containers). Most files don't care, but two classes of
# files are byte-sensitive to this difference and must be pinned instead
# of left to the platform default.

# Frozen Eval v3 hash-verified artifacts.
# The expected SHA-256 values in
# benchmark/reports/frozen-eval-v3-opus5-thinking-hashes.txt were computed
# against CRLF bytes (the representation these files have always had on a
# Windows checkout). The git blobs themselves store LF, so a Linux
# checkout previously materialized LF bytes that didn't match those
# published hashes and benchmark/eval/frozen_v3_replay.py failed closed
# with "one or more Frozen Eval v3 artifact hashes changed". Pinning
# eol=crlf makes checkout produce the same CRLF bytes the hashes were
# computed from, on every OS, with no change to the stored git objects or
# the published hash values.
benchmark/reports/frozen-eval-v3-opus5-thinking-operational-raw-240.json text eol=crlf
benchmark/reports/frozen-eval-v3-opus5-thinking-provider-recovered-240.json text eol=crlf
fixtures/trajectories/frozen-eval-v3-opus5-thinking-final/*.json text eol=crlf
fixtures/trajectories/frozen-eval-v3-opus5-thinking-t2-provider-failures-original/*.json text eol=crlf

# Shell entrypoints must always check out as LF. A CRLF shebang line
# ("#!/bin/sh\r") is not a valid interpreter path on Linux, which is what
# produced "exec /usr/local/bin/finrecon-entrypoint: no such file or
# directory" in the container despite the COPY succeeding.
*.sh text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
